An xref entry that names the wrong object was trusted anyway. One stray row in
an xref stream (seen after pdf-lib stamped a form and rewrote the Catalog)
shifts every later entry onto its neighbour's offset, so an ObjStm lookup lands
on an image XObject and the whole document fails with "expected /Type /ObjStm,
got /Type /XObject". When an entry's header names another object, or its
stream isn't an object stream holding the object, find the object where it
really is (the whole-file header scan, then the object-stream sweep) before
falling back to the old outcome.

The sweep now runs for more files, so it no longer resurrects superseded
revisions: it skips objects whose uncompressed xref entry checks out, prefers
the stream the xref names (else the newest copy), and only treats an object as
an ObjStm candidate when /Type /ObjStm is in its own dictionary.

